WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.019
Picture a small cafe that keeps its count of customers on a sticky note, stuck to the till.

2
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:12.420
Every night, the cleaners swap the till for a fresh one, note and all.

3
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.351
So every morning, the count starts again at zero.

4
00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:18.747
So you move the count into a notebook on the back shelf.

5
00:00:18.747 --> 00:00:20.679
It does not go away with the till.

6
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.210
The next morning a new cashier arrives.

7
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.756
And nobody has told her where the shelf is.

8
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:27.319
Two separate problems.

9
00:00:27.319 --> 00:00:29.532
Where to keep things that must last.

10
00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:31.500
And how helpers find each other.

11
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.233
It is easy to fix one and forget the other, so let's take them one at a time.

12
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.272
In Docker, the till is a container.

13
00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:44.037
The sticky note is its own thin writable layer, where it keeps what it writes.

14
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:48.270
And the cleaners are docker rm, followed by a fresh container.

15
00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.369
You do that on every upgrade, and after a crash.

16
00:00:51.369 --> 00:00:56.276
So our visit count from hello-devops started at zero in every new container.

17
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.419
That is fine.

18
00:00:57.419 --> 00:01:00.812
You want to be able to throw any container away.

19
00:01:00.812 --> 00:01:02.226
So here is the rule.

20
00:01:02.226 --> 00:01:04.771
Treat every container as disposable.

21
00:01:04.771 --> 00:01:06.539
Keep the data outside it.

22
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:12.114
The back shelf is a volume, storage that Docker creates and looks after.

23
00:01:12.114 --> 00:01:15.344
We mount it at one folder inside the container.

24
00:01:15.344 --> 00:01:17.405
Here, nginx's web page folder.

25
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:24.531
A throwaway container writes our own page into the volume, and nginx serves it: served from a volume.

26
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:26.152
Now the cleaners come.

27
00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:31.055
We throw the container away, and start a brand new one with the same volume.

28
00:01:31.055 --> 00:01:32.603
Is the page still there?

29
00:01:32.603 --> 00:01:33.635
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:36.148
Yes.

31
00:01:36.148 --> 00:01:43.453
A new container, with a new ID, and the same page, because the page lives on the shelf, not in the till.

32
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:49.081
Docker has three kinds of these mounts, and you pick by asking who owns the data.

33
00:01:49.081 --> 00:01:52.423
Docker owns it, like a database's files: a volume.

34
00:01:52.423 --> 00:01:57.035
You own it, like code you edit: a bind mount, a folder from your Mac.

35
00:01:57.035 --> 00:02:00.845
Nobody should keep it: tmpfs, which lives in memory only.

36
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:03.975
Now the new cashier.

37
00:02:03.975 --> 00:02:11.935
Every container you start plain joins Docker's default network, a virtual switch called the bridge.

38
00:02:11.935 --> 00:02:18.045
Each one gets an IP address, its number on that network, such as 172.17.0.5.

39
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:20.147
By that number, a can reach b.

40
00:02:20.147 --> 00:02:24.787
But numbers change when containers restart, so you cannot write them down.

41
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.610
You might think: just use localhost, the name that means this machine.

42
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.466
Let's try it.

43
00:02:30.466 --> 00:02:33.297
Tell the app that Redis lives at localhost.

44
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.305
But inside a container, localhost is the container itself.

45
00:02:37.305 --> 00:02:41.897
The app knocks on its own door, where nothing listens on Redis's port.

46
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.435
It is the cashier phoning herself to ask where the shelf is.

47
00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:47.296
So we want names.

48
00:02:47.296 --> 00:02:53.442
From a container on the default bridge, ask for our nginx container by its name, web.

49
00:02:53.442 --> 00:02:54.599
Pause and guess.

50
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.777
Bad address.

51
00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:01.116
The default bridge has no lookup by name.

52
00:03:01.116 --> 00:03:02.175
Numbers only.

53
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.809
Now make a network of our own.

54
00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:06.616
docker network create demo.

55
00:03:06.616 --> 00:03:10.031
Then start web on it, and our asking container too.

56
00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:16.545
A network you create comes with something the default bridge lacks.

57
00:03:16.545 --> 00:03:17.673
A staff list.

58
00:03:17.673 --> 00:03:27.649
Docker's own DNS server, the service that turns names into addresses, waiting inside every container at 127.0.0.11.

59
00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:35.631
It knows every name on this network, because Docker handed out the addresses in the first place.

60
00:03:35.631 --> 00:03:38.867
Ask for web, and it answers: 172.23.0.2.

61
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.409
Same request, from the demo network.

62
00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:46.413
The name goes to the staff list, the address comes back, and the page arrives.

63
00:03:46.413 --> 00:03:47.760
Served from a volume.

64
00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:49.362
So here is the rule.

65
00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:53.829
Containers find each other by name only on a network you create.

66
00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:55.155
Create one per app.

67
00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:56.951
One more surprise.

68
00:03:56.951 --> 00:04:02.414
Containers on the same network reach each other on any port, with no dash p at all.

69
00:04:02.414 --> 00:04:07.349
Publishing with dash p is only for traffic from outside, like your browser.

70
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:08.933
And the honest limits.

71
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:12.599
A volume stays on the one Docker machine where you made it.

72
00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:17.507
And every container still needs its own long command, typed in the right order.

73
00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:19.654
So, back to the cafe.

74
00:04:19.654 --> 00:04:24.188
The count lives on the back shelf, a volume that outlives any till.

75
00:04:24.188 --> 00:04:30.008
And the new cashier reads the staff list, the network's DNS, to find everyone by name.

76
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:31.451
Quick check.

77
00:04:31.451 --> 00:04:35.334
Inside the web container, REDIS_HOST is set to localhost.

78
00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:37.651
Where does the app look for Redis?

79
00:04:39.867 --> 00:04:40.638
At itself.

80
00:04:40.638 --> 00:04:45.725
Put both on one network you created, and use Redis's name instead.
